    "narrative": "With the 2026 stretch run intensifying, the Zurich Zebras lean on **Trea Turner** and **Kazuma Okamoto** to anchor today\u2019s offensive output. **Trea Turner** remains our premier play with a 78.06 efficiency score; facing Holmes, a right-hander with a pedestrian 4.72 xERA, **Trea Turner** gains significant utility from his #2 spot in the order and a favorable park factor, positioning him as our primary engine for stolen bases and runs. Meanwhile, **Kazuma Okamoto** earns a 69.87 rating, buoyed by an ideal platoon advantage against Lopez and a park factor boost that rewards his power profile. **Christian Encarnacion-Strand** also cracks the 67-point threshold, utilizing a strong matchup against the southpaw Cantillo to overcome his spot lower in the order, offering reliable upside in the RBI category.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was **Dillon Dingler**, who posted a 36.68 efficiency score and narrowly missed our 40-point start threshold. While the matchup against Ryan offered some park-based optimism, the combination of a significant platoon penalty against a right-handed arm and his lower standing in the lineup pushed **Dillon Dingler** below our internal floor, favoring the higher-ceiling profiles of **Caleb Durbin** and **Pete Crow-Armstrong**. We remain cautious regarding **Pete Crow-Armstrong**'s 59.63 score; although he is a priority for his elite sprint speed against Gasser, the -15% platoon hit for a left-on-left matchup is a notable drag on his overall projection, even if Gasser\u2019s slow delivery offers a prime opportunity for **Pete Crow-Armstrong** to swipe a base.\n\nHeading into the afternoon, we are monitoring the risk factors surrounding **Colt Keith** and **Christian Yelich**, both of whom are hovering near the bottom of our active range. **Colt Keith** faces a tough test against Ryan\u2019s 3.41 xERA, where his favorable order placement is currently battling a 14% skill deficit against the pitcher. Similarly, **Christian Yelich** is fighting against an elite hold-pitcher in Boyd, whose tendency to suppress run games limits the upside for **Christian Yelich** today. While **Yordan Alvarez**, **Junior Caminero**, **Gary Sanchez**, **Lars Nootbaar**, **Casey Schmitt**, **Josh Smith**, **Chase Meidroth**, **Jacob Gonzalez**, **Hector Rodriguez**, **Luis Pena**, and **Pedro Ramirez** are either inactive or on rest, the current lineup offers the best statistical probability to maintain our rotation velocity while prioritizing the stolen base potential we desperately need.",
